        },
        "text": "@cvpayne  $NVDA  :  All seven are buying Nvidia's anyway.  (shows the shift is additive rather than a full substitution)\nThey are. The dual strategy is deliberate: custom silicon for high-volume, predictable internal workloads (especially inference) plus continued large-scale Nvidia purchases for flexibility, frontier training, external cloud customers, and rapid capacity ramps.⁠ Axios⁠Coinpaper\n\nNvidia’s edge is less pure silicon performance at this point and more the full stack.  CUDA and the surrounding software ecosystem remain sticky for most developers and third-party cloud users; porting large codebases is expensive and slow.  GPUs are also general-purpose, so hyperscalers can reallocate them across changing models and customer needs.  Custom ASICs (Google’s TPUs/Ironwood, Amazon’s Trainium, Microsoft’s Maia, Meta’s MTIA, OpenAI’s Jalapeño with Broadcom, and Anthropic’s efforts) are typically optimized for specific, stable workloads where the total cost of ownership advantage can be material once amortized. \n\nSemiAnalysis’s framing is consistent with the broader picture: the six customers building their own chips still account for a huge share of Nvidia GPU deployments, while Nvidia’s support for neoclouds (CoreWeave and others) keeps an alternative supply channel open so hyperscalers do not fully control the market. Custom chips reduce dependence over time, particularly on inference, but the data through late 2026 shows the shift is additive rather than a full substitution.⁠ Rcrtech"
      },
